calibre: keep the entry names of an SNB book as text so the reader finds snbf/book.snbf

calibre 8.5.0 (the Debian package of the worker image) stores each entry name of an SNB
container as bytes and looks it up with text names, so its SNB reader finds no entry: it
writes an empty book and stops with "IndexError: list index out of range" (docs/policy-decisions.md,
entries 309 and 465). The change below is upstream's own fix, taken unmodified and applied to
the installed snbfile.py while the image is built (Dockerfile.worker). calibre runs as a
separate program; nothing of it is linked into or copied to src/.

Upstream commit:  https://github.com/kovidgoyal/calibre/commit/c33f0a54bd63ac25fb3009ab5ac587a62dacfd80
                  c33f0a54bd63ac25fb3009ab5ac587a62dacfd80 "SNB input: fix IndexError by keeping entry
                  filenames as str", by dipu-bd, 2026-06-19, first released in v9.10.0
File:             src/calibre/ebooks/snb/snbfile.py (calibre 8.5.0 .. 9.5.0 lack the change)
Licence:          GPL-3.0 (`__license__ = 'GPL v3'` in the file; calibre COPYRIGHT file: GPL-3), Copyright (C) 2008-2026 Kovid
                  Goyal and contributors; docs/licensing.md, section 2.41
Applies to:       calibre 8.5.0+ds-1+deb13u3 with an offset of one line and no fuzz; the build skips it from calibre 9.10

The text above the first "diff" line is ignored by patch(1).

diff --git a/src/calibre/ebooks/snb/snbfile.py b/src/calibre/ebooks/snb/snbfile.py
index 2ad97261cdcc..1ad422980507 100644
--- a/src/calibre/ebooks/snb/snbfile.py
+++ b/src/calibre/ebooks/snb/snbfile.py
@@ -107,7 +107,8 @@ def ParseFile(self, vfat, fileCount):
         for i in range(fileCount):
             f = FileStream()
             (f.attr, f.fileNameOffset, f.fileSize) = struct.unpack('>iii', vfat[i * 12 : (i+1)*12])
-            f.fileName = fileNames[i]
+            # Decode to str so lookups against str keys match (see GetFileStream)
+            f.fileName = fileNames[i].decode('utf-8', 'replace')
             self.files.append(f)
 
     def ParseTail(self, vtail, fileCount):
@@ -159,8 +160,6 @@ def AppendPlain(self, fileName, tdir):
         with open(os.path.join(tdir, fileName), 'rb') as data:
             f.fileBody = data.read()
         f.fileName = fileName.replace(os.sep, '/')
-        if isinstance(f.fileName, str):
-            f.fileName = f.fileName.encode('ascii', 'ignore')
         self.files.append(f)
 
     def AppendBinary(self, fileName, tdir):
@@ -170,8 +169,6 @@ def AppendBinary(self, fileName, tdir):
         with open(os.path.join(tdir, fileName), 'rb') as data:
             f.fileBody = data.read()
         f.fileName = fileName.replace(os.sep, '/')
-        if isinstance(f.fileName, str):
-            f.fileName = f.fileName.encode('ascii', 'ignore')
         self.files.append(f)
 
     def GetFileStream(self, fileName):
@@ -208,7 +205,7 @@ def Output(self, outputFile):
         binStream = b''
         for f in self.files:
             vfat += struct.pack('>iii', f.attr, len(fileNameTable), f.fileSize)
-            fileNameTable += (f.fileName + b'\0')
+            fileNameTable += f.fileName.encode('ascii', 'ignore') + b'\0'
 
             if f.attr & 0x41000000 == 0x41000000:
                 # Plain Files
